<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/gulf-coast-harbor.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/gulf-coast-harbor-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/gulf-coast-harbor.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/counties-and-cities/">Counties &amp; cities</a></li><li aria-current="page">Gulfport</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Gulf Coast</p><h1>Gulfport Hurricane and Wind Claim Appraisal</h1><p class="lede">On the Coast, one storm can mean two or three policies, a percentage deductible and a fight over wind versus water. If you and your carrier can’t agree on the amount of your Gulfport loss, appraisal is one way to settle it.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="hero-facts"><li><small>County</small><b>Harrison County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>Gulf Coast</b></li><li><small>Inspections</small><b>On site</b></li></ul></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><div class="glance">
<div><p class="glance-h">Neighborhoods &amp; areas</p><ul class="chips"><li>Downtown Gulfport</li><li>Handsboro</li><li>Orange Grove</li><li>Bayou View</li><li>North Gulfport</li><li>Long Beach</li></ul></div>
<div><p class="glance-h">Housing mix</p><p>Gulfport has raised and elevated homes rebuilt to post-Katrina standards near the beach, older cottages and brick homes in established neighborhoods, and newer subdivisions to the north toward Interstate 10.</p></div>
<div><p class="glance-h">Storm history</p><ul class="storm-list"><li><b>Aug 17, 1969</b><span>Hurricane Camille’s Category 5 landfall to the west near Pass Christian</span></li><li><b>Aug 29, 2005</b><span>Hurricane Katrina’s surge and wind</span></li><li><b>Aug 2012</b><span>Hurricane Isaac’s rain and surge</span></li><li><b>Oct 28, 2020</b><span>Hurricane Zeta’s strong wind across the Coast</span></li></ul></div>
<div><p class="glance-h">Local claim risks</p><ul class="chips chips-red"><li>Hurricane wind and storm surge</li><li>Split homeowners and MWUA wind policies</li><li>Named-storm percentage deductibles</li><li>Roof and siding damage from tropical storms</li><li>Flood losses under separate NFIP policies</li></ul></div>
</div><h2>Gulfport, Harrison County and the split-policy problem</h2>
<p>Gulfport is one of Harrison County’s two county seats (Biloxi is the other) and home to the state port. Harrison County is one of the six southernmost counties where the Mississippi Windstorm Underwriting Association (MWUA) writes wind and hail coverage. Many Gulfport owners carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind, an MWUA wind policy, and a separate flood policy, usually through the NFIP.</p>
<p>That arrangement shapes every storm claim. Each carrier writes its own estimate, applies its own deductible and tends to point at the other policy for the damage in between.</p>

<h2>Katrina, Zeta and what the Coast learned</h2>
<p>Hurricane Katrina’s surge on August 29, 2005 pushed far inland across Gulfport, Long Beach and Pass Christian, and wind damage reached well past the surge line. It produced years of wind-versus-water disputes and arguments over anti-concurrent-causation clauses. Our guide on <a href="/claim-advice/wind-vs-water-after-katrina/">wind vs. water after Katrina</a> walks through that history.</p>
<p>More recent storms have been smaller but still costly. Hurricane Isaac in August 2012 brought heavy rain and surge. Hurricane Zeta made landfall in Louisiana on October 28, 2020, but drove strong wind across the Mississippi Coast, tearing shingles, soffits and fences in Gulfport neighborhoods.</p>
<div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Coverage vs. amount</b><p>Whether damage was caused by wind or by water is often a coverage question, and appraisal does not decide coverage. Appraisal can, however, set the dollar amount of the wind damage, or of each part of a loss, when the policy allows it.</p></div>

<h2>Gulfport homes and the estimates they produce</h2>
<p>Areas like Handsboro, Orange Grove, Bayou View and Downtown Gulfport show the whole range: historic cottages, mid-century brick homes, and the raised homes built after 2005 on piers or pilings. North Gulfport and the subdivisions along the Interstate 10 corridor are newer and mostly slab-on-grade.</p>
<p>The disputes we see most often on Gulfport claims:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deductible math:</strong> a 2% to 5% named-storm deductible on Coverage A can wipe out a partial-roof estimate. Our <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/named-storm-deductible-calculator/">named-storm deductible calculator</a> shows where your number lands.</li>
<li><strong>Elevated-home access:</strong> raised homes need lifts, scaffolding and longer labor time. Carrier estimates often price them like a one-story slab house.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades:</strong> repairs may trigger current wind-load and elevation requirements. Ordinance or law coverage, if you have it, may pay for those.</li>
<li><strong>Partial roof replacement:</strong> when matching shingles aren’t available, a patch leaves a roof that doesn’t match.</li>
<li><strong>Allocation:</strong> interior damage gets assigned to flood when the roof opening says wind.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When appraisal fits a Gulfport claim</h2>
<p>Appraisal is most useful when coverage is accepted and the fight is over price and scope. Each policy has its own appraisal clause, so a wind-policy appraisal and a homeowners-policy appraisal can be separate tracks. Read the clause first. Our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/hurricane-named-storm-appraisal/">hurricane and named-storm appraisal</a> service covers how we handle split-policy claims.</p>
<p>We serve Long Beach and Pass Christian as well, and the rest of Harrison County. Policy and legal deadlines keep running during a dispute, so confirm yours with a Mississippi attorney.</p>
<h2>Appraisal services in Gulfport</h2>
<p>Every Mississippi Insurance Appraiser service is available in Gulfport and the rest of Harrison County, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/roof-hail-claim-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/hurricane-named-storm-appraisal/">Hurricane &amp; Named Storm Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/carrier-estimate-review/">Carrier Estimate Review</a></li></ul>
<h2>Gulfport claim questions</h2><div class="faqs"><details><summary>Can I demand appraisal on my MWUA wind policy?</summary><p>If your MWUA policy contains an appraisal clause, you can generally use it for a dispute over the amount of a covered wind loss. Read the clause for its exact steps and deadlines, and keep in mind that each policy is handled separately.</p></details><details><summary>Does appraisal decide whether my Gulfport damage was wind or flood?</summary><p>No. Whether wind or water caused a loss is generally a coverage question. Appraisal sets the amount of loss, and in some cases the amount of each part of a loss, which can still help narrow a wind-versus-water dispute.</p></details></div>
